 "chunks": [
  {
   "text": "So where are we?",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 1.75
  },
  {
   "text": "What the Lean and Mathlib statement certifies is the classical Riemann Hypothesis.",
   "start": 1.75,
   "end": 6.312
  },
  {
   "text": "Andrew Sutherland's verification page at MIT reports that the library also proves",
   "start": 6.312,
   "end": 10.762
  },
  {
   "text": "the same on-the-line statement for every Dirichlet L-function:",
   "start": 10.762,
   "end": 14.25
  },
  {
   "text": "the generalized hypothesis, in that sense.",
   "start": 14.25,
   "end": 17.1
  },
  {
   "text": "Wider versions, for other kinds of L-functions, haven't been checked;",
   "start": 17.1,
   "end": 20.912
  },
  {
   "text": "Alpöge only expects one, the extended hypothesis,",
   "start": 20.912,
   "end": 24.25
  },
  {
   "text": "with more time.",
   "start": 24.25,
   "end": 25.45
  },
  {
   "text": "And there's no human-readable proof yet; he says he'll write it properly.",
   "start": 25.45,
   "end": 29.512
  },
  {
   "text": "And the Clay prize requires publication, then two years,",
   "start": 29.975,
   "end": 33.45
  },
  {
   "text": "then general acceptance.",
   "start": 33.45,
   "end": 35.2
  },
  {
   "text": "So for now, it's a claimed proof: checked by machines,",
   "start": 36.125,
   "end": 39.775
  },
  {
   "text": "not yet explained in human terms.",
   "start": 39.775,
   "end": 42.225
  },
  {
   "text": "If it holds, the zeros really do all sit on one line,",
   "start": 42.225,
   "end": 45.8
  },
  {
   "text": "and the reason may be an old idea, counting with multiplicity,",
   "start": 45.8,
   "end": 49.475
  },
  {
   "text": "finally made to work for the whole numbers.",
   "start": 49.475,
   "end": 51.95
  }
 ],
 "cues": {
  "certified": 1.75,
  "dirichlet": 6.312,
  "notyet": 17.1,
  "clay": 29.975,
  "close": 36.125
 }
